  "query_preview": "..Today...A cold front has move eastward across \nArizona as the parent upper-level low propagates eastward. Drier \nair has been ushered into much of the state as of this morning, \nwith dewpoints falling to into the 30s F and 40s F across northern\nArizona. PWAT has also dropped, with the FGZ RAOB falling from \n0.72 inches at 18Z to 0.53 inches by 00Z. As such, cooler and \ndrier conditions are expected to prevail across the region today.\n\nWhile dry air has moved in, flooding on area waterways remains a \nthreat through this morning after the prolonged rainfall from the \npast 48 hours. Water levels should gradually fall through this \nmorning, but will likely still remain elevated into much of the day \ntoday.\n\nSaturday and Sunday...A fragment of the aforementioned upper-level \nlow looks to break off from the jet, becoming a closed-low over \nArizona over the weekend. Cool temperatures look to remain, but \nprecipitation chances look to creep back into the region. The most \nlikely chances for rain and potentially an isolated thunderstorm \nwill mainly be over the higher terrain on Saturday, shifting into \neastern Arizona on Sunday.\n\nMonday through Thursday...As the closed-low lifts into Colorado, \nweak ridging, or even quasi-zonal flow looks to build into the \nsouthwest. Gradually warming temperatures and dry conditions look to \nprevail into much of next week. A deepening trough over the Pacific \nNorthwest looks to potentially breakdown this pattern near the end \nof next week, however model guidance remains uncertain in the exact \ntrack of the low.",
